const NATIONAL_GAS_LATEST={price:"4.483",asOf:"Sep 24, 2026"};
const US_DIESEL_LATEST={price:"6.514",asOf:"Sep 24, 2026"};
const BRIEFING={summaryShort:"Russia pressed its air war on Kyiv into daylight on Sep 25: after 295 drones overnight (267 downed), strikes on a Pecherskyi high-rise and a Solomianskyi office building killed 7, including a 14-year-old, and injured at least 46, per Mayor Klitschko. Ukraine hit refineries in Perm and Novoshakhtinsk, which halted operations, and Zelensky said Trump had made a ‘final decision’ to license Patriot production, which the White House has not confirmed. On Iran, Araghchi offered to reopen Hormuz within seven days if the US lifts its blockade and oil sanctions; Washington called the exchanges constructive but said there is no rush, and Brent settled at $104.61, down 1.9%. Chinese cutters and warships blocked a Philippine resupply to Second Thomas Shoal on Sep 24, the first reported blockade since the 2024 arrangement.",summary:"The day’s heaviest toll was in Kyiv. After an overnight wave of 295 drones, of which the Air Force said 267 were downed, Russian drones kept hitting the capital in daylight: a jet-powered Shahed struck a Pecherskyi high-rise around 8am, killing a 14-year-old boy, and an afternoon strike on a Solomianskyi office building killed 4. Klitschko put the toll at 7 killed and 46 injured by early evening; the Kyiv Independent counted 57 injured by 8pm. Ukraine struck back overnight at the Perm and Novoshakhtinsk refineries, the latter suspending operations, and at Almaz-Antey’s Iskra plant in Ulyanovsk — The Moscow Times, Ukrinform. Ukraine’s General Staff puts cumulative Russian losses at 1,525,570 (+1,590). Diplomacy moved at the margins. Zelensky said Trump told him he had made a ‘final decision’ to let Ukraine produce Patriot missiles under license, reversing the July walk-back, though the White House had not commented; CNN reported that Putin envoy Dmitriev met Witkoff and Kushner in New York on Sep 24, and Zelensky said US-proposed technical talks, possibly in the UAE, should be settled within ten days. Macron said France sees ‘several signs’ Russia is preparing to mobilize 300,000 more men, which the Kremlin denies. On Iran, Araghchi pitched a compressed seven-day plan on the UNGA sidelines: Hormuz reopens at the end of day seven if the US lifts its blockade and oil sanctions, releases frozen assets and a ceasefire holds on all fronts including Lebanon. Rubio said it would be wrong to call the talks a breakthrough, and a White House official said there is no rush — AP, Al Jazeera, CBS News. Brent settled at $104.61, down 1.86% — Trading Economics. Saudi, Turkish and Pakistani military chiefs met in Riyadh under their mutual-defense pact after Houthi missile fire on Taif and Yanbu. Elsewhere, three China Coast Guard cutters and three PLA Navy warships forced a Philippine resupply mission to Second Thomas Shoal to turn back on Sep 24 — USNI, Reuters. At the UN, Pakistan’s Sharif said diverting the Indus would be ‘an act of war,’ Lebanon’s Salam demanded full Israeli withdrawal, and Haiti’s Fils-Aimé pressed for the gang-suppression force ahead of its Sep 30 renewal.",assessment:[{cat:"Russia Is Extending the Air War Into Daylight Hours",text:"Strikes continuing through the morning and afternoon after a near-300-drone night keep air defenses and civilians under pressure for most of the day, and the Kyiv toll shows that daylight hits on populated districts now carry the highest cost."},{cat:"Both Big Negotiating Tracks Are Moving Without a Deadline",text:"Iran’s seven-day offer and the Patriot-licence and technical-talks news give Washington new openings with Tehran and Kyiv, but US officials’ ‘no rush’ line and the lack of White House confirmation leave both tracks without a forcing date."},{cat:"The South China Sea Arrangement Has Been Tested",text:"The first reported blockade of a Second Thomas Shoal resupply since July 2024 puts the provisional arrangement in doubt and adds a new flashpoint only a day after the Trump–Xi summit."}],watch:"Whether Russia repeats daylight strikes on Kyiv and whether the toll rises; White House confirmation or denial of the Patriot-licence decision; a date and venue for US-proposed technical talks; Washington’s formal answer to Iran’s seven-day plan and whether Brent holds below $105; Lavrov’s Sep 26 UN address; Manila’s response and the next resupply attempt at Second Thomas Shoal; and the Sep 30 UN Security Council vote on Haiti’s gang force.",generatedAt:REPORT_NOW.toISOString()};
const UKRAINE_BRIEFING={summaryShort:"Russian drones killed 7 people in Kyiv on Sep 25, including a 14-year-old in a Pecherskyi high-rise, and injured at least 46, per Mayor Klitschko, as strikes continued into daylight after 295 drones overnight (267 downed). Ukraine hit the Perm and Novoshakhtinsk refineries, the latter halting operations, and the Iskra plant in Ulyanovsk. Zelensky said Trump had made a ‘final decision’ to license Patriot production, which the White House has not confirmed; Dmitriev met Witkoff and Kushner on Sep 24. Russian losses stand at 1,525,570 (+1,590).",summary:"Russia’s air campaign pushed into daylight on Sep 25. Overnight it launched 295 drones, 52 of them jet-powered Shaheds; the Air Force said 267 were downed or suppressed, with hits at 16 locations. Strikes on Kyiv then continued through the day: a jet-powered Shahed hit a Pecherskyi high-rise around 8am, killing a 14-year-old boy, and an afternoon strike on a Solomianskyi office building killed 4. Klitschko put the capital’s toll at 7 killed and 46 injured by early evening, and the Kyiv Independent counted 57 injured by 8pm — Ukrinform, Kyiv Independent. Elsewhere, one person was killed in Odesa region and a hospital building in Zaporizhzhia was damaged — Meduza, RFE/RL. This followed the Sep 23–24 Zircon and ballistic wave that damaged a Kyiv maternity hospital and a drone strike that killed 6–7 farm workers in Kharkiv region. Ukraine’s deep-strike campaign continued: overnight Sep 24–25 drones hit the Lukoil refinery in Perm and the Novoshakhtinsk refinery in Rostov Oblast, which suspended operations, plus Almaz-Antey’s Iskra plant in Ulyanovsk; Zelensky said two Russian radars were destroyed in Rostov region, while Russia claimed 592 drones downed — The Moscow Times, Ukrinform. On diplomacy, Zelensky said Trump told him he had made a ‘final decision’ to let Ukraine produce Patriot missiles under license, reversing the July walk-back of the Ankara offer; the White House had not commented. CNN reported Putin envoy Dmitriev met Witkoff and Kushner in New York on Sep 24, and Zelensky said details of US-proposed technical talks, possibly in the UAE, should be settled within ten days. Macron said France sees ‘several signs’ Russia is preparing to mobilize 300,000 more men now that the Duma election is over; the Kremlin says there are no such plans. Ukraine’s General Staff put cumulative Russian losses at 1,525,570 (+1,590), after +1,560 on Sep 23 and +1,540 on Sep 24 — Ukrainska Pravda, Ukrinform. On the ground, ISW assessed that neither side made confirmed advances on Sep 24 and that Moscow keeps making unsubstantiated claims of encircling Dobropillya. Russia’s 2027–29 budget draft, released Sep 24, now expects a 2026 deficit near 3% of GDP and adds new taxes, even as Urals trades around $110.",assessment:[{cat:"Daylight Strikes Raise the Civilian Cost",text:"Following a near-300-drone night with hours of daytime hits on residential and office buildings keeps Kyiv under alert for most of the day and produced the capital’s deadliest day of the week."},{cat:"The Patriot Decision Matters Only if It Is Confirmed",text:"A licence for domestic Patriot production would address Ukraine’s most binding air-defense constraint, but after July’s walk-back, the claim needs White House confirmation and a production timeline before it changes the picture."},{cat:"Mobilization Signals and Budget Strain Point in Opposite Directions",text:"Western warnings of a 300,000-man call-up suggest Moscow is preparing for a longer war, while a widening deficit and new taxes show the cost rising even with Urals near $110 — Ukraine’s refinery strikes add to that pressure."}],watch:"Whether the Kyiv toll rises and daylight strikes recur; White House confirmation of the Patriot-licence decision; the date and venue of US-proposed technical talks; Lavrov’s Sep 26 UN General Assembly speech; any mobilization decree in Moscow; the scale of refinery outages after the Perm and Novoshakhtinsk hits; and further drone spillover into Moldova, Romania or Poland.",generatedAt:REPORT_NOW.toISOString()};
const IRAN_BRIEFING={summaryShort:"Iran has compressed its offer: on the UNGA sidelines FM Araghchi said Hormuz would reopen at the end of day seven if the US lifts its naval blockade and oil sanctions, releases frozen assets and a ceasefire holds on all fronts including Lebanon. Rubio said it would be wrong to call the talks a breakthrough, and a White House official said there is no rush. Pezeshkian said Iran wants a deal before the US midterms and is willing to dilute its enriched uranium. Brent settled at $104.61, down 1.9%; Saudi, Turkish and Pakistani military chiefs met in Riyadh after Houthi fire on Taif and Yanbu.",summary:"The diplomatic track has a new, shorter offer on the table. At a private gathering on the UNGA sidelines, Araghchi said the strait would reopen ‘at the end of the seventh day’ if the US lifts its naval blockade, waives oil sanctions, releases frozen assets and a ceasefire holds on all fronts including Lebanon, with nuclear talks to follow — a compressed version of the June memorandum and of Iran’s Sep 23 60-day road map. Iran wants the strait handled first and the nuclear file later, while Washington wants all issues discussed together. Rubio said it would be wrong to call the discussions a breakthrough; a White House official called them ‘positive and constructive’ but said there is no rush — AP, Al Jazeera, CBS News. No second formal round has been announced, and the impeachment motion against Araghchi in Tehran has not advanced publicly. Pezeshkian told reporters and Fox News that Iran wants the US ‘to return to the MOU before the midterms,’ that it is ‘willing to dilute’ its enriched uranium under verification, and that he met Supreme Leader Mojtaba Khamenei for seven hours in August — Fox News, Jerusalem Post. Around the strait, transits remain a trickle: 11 on Sep 23 per Windward and 13 on Sep 24 per The National, against a pre-war ~110 a day. The Houthi front with Saudi Arabia is widening: after the Sep 24 missile fire on Taif and Yanbu, the military chiefs of Saudi Arabia, Turkey and Pakistan met in Riyadh under their mutual-defense pact and named its first secretary-general, and a Houthi official warned Pakistan and Turkey against joining the war — The New Arab, Al Jazeera. US aviation sanctions are biting: Baghdad, Najaf, Erbil and Sulaimaniyah airports halted Iranian flights, following the UAE and Qatar. Brent settled at $104.61 on Sep 25, down 1.86% on the day, after $106.60 on Sep 24 — Trading Economics.",assessment:[{cat:"Tehran Is Trying to Separate Hormuz From the Nuclear File",text:"The seven-day plan offers Washington the strait quickly in exchange for sanctions relief first, while deferring nuclear talks; the US preference for a single package is now the main gap, not whether Iran will talk at all."},{cat:"Washington Is Signaling Patience, Not Urgency",text:"With oil moving under escort and Brent below $105, US officials see little cost in waiting; that reduces the pressure for a quick answer and gives Tehran’s hardliners time to contest the channel."},{cat:"The Saudi Front Is Drawing In New Partners",text:"Pakistani and Turkish military chiefs meeting in Riyadh after Houthi strikes on Saudi soil, and Houthi threats against both, show how the Yanbu attacks risk widening the war beyond the US–Iran axis."}],watch:"Washington’s formal answer to the seven-day plan and whether a second round is scheduled; any movement on the Araghchi impeachment motion; whether Brent stays below $105; Houthi fire on Yanbu or Taif and any Pakistani or Turkish deployment under the Riyadh pact; further airports closing to Iranian carriers; and daily Hormuz transit counts.",generatedAt:REPORT_NOW.toISOString()};
const BELARUS_BRIEFING={summaryShort:"Minsk’s prisoners-for-sanctions track is producing steady increments: Lukashenko pardoned 15 prisoners on Sep 24, per state agency Belta via Reuters, a week after a Trump envoy announced 25 political-prisoner releases tied to US sanctions easing; Meduza, citing Belta, says 13 of the 15 were convicted of ‘extremist’ crimes and 10 are women; names were not released. Poland’s Sikorski held the first high-level talks with Belarus’s foreign minister in a decade on Sep 23 and plans a new embassy building in Minsk. On the military side, Belarus remains an enabler of Russia’s air war — Kyiv says Belarusian LTE and repeater networks help guide Shaheds — but no new border incident has been reported since the Sep 15–18 drills, and the Sep 15 drone shootdown over Lithuania remains unattributed.",summary:"Belarus continues to run a western-facing thaw alongside its role in Russia’s war. On the diplomatic track, Lukashenko pardoned 15 prisoners on Sep 24, per Belta via Reuters, and Meduza, citing Belta, reports that 13 of the 15 were convicted of what Belarus calls ‘extremist’ crimes, 10 are women and all petitioned and admitted guilt; names were not released, and Viasna says more than 800 political prisoners remain; it came after Trump envoy John Coale’s visit to Minsk the previous week and a Sep 16 announcement that Belarus had freed 25 political prisoners in exchange for US sanctions easing, extending the arrangement under which Washington lifted potash sanctions in Dec 2025 for more than 100 releases. Trump talked up a ‘massive’ potash purchase on Sep 21; Lukashenko replied that Belarus has no spare volume. On Sep 23 Poland’s Radosław Sikorski met Belarusian FM Maxim Ryzhenkov at the UN — the first high-level Polish–Belarusian talks in a decade — and said Poland will soon open a new embassy building in Minsk while recognizing ‘states, not governments.’ On the military track, Ukrainian officials say Russia uses Belarusian LTE networks and repeater stations to keep Shaheds connected, and exiled opposition leader Sviatlana Tsikhanouskaya said Sep 23 that over 500 Belarusian companies supply Russia’s war effort and that new rail infrastructure in Gomel Oblast can receive military trains. Along the NATO border, the Sep 15–18 exercises in the Brest and Grodno regions ended with no follow-on drill and no further incident; the drone NATO jets downed over Lithuania on Sep 15 remains formally unattributed. The regional backdrop darkened on Sep 24 when Denmark’s military intelligence assessed a ‘low but growing’ risk of a limited Russian attack on a NATO border state — a scenario in which Belarusian territory would matter.",assessment:[{cat:"Releases Are Arriving in Small, Regular Tranches",text:"Twenty-five releases announced Sep 16 and 15 pardons on Sep 24 fit a pattern of incremental concessions timed to US engagement, letting Minsk extract sanctions relief step by step without a single large deal it would have to defend in Moscow."},{cat:"The Thaw Is Western-Facing, Not a Realignment",text:"Telecom support for Shahed guidance, military-capable rail in Gomel and hundreds of suppliers to Russian industry continue regardless of the prisoner track; Warsaw’s ‘states, not governments’ caveat shows partners understand the limit."},{cat:"Denmark’s Warning Raises the Stakes of a Quiet Border",text:"With no incident since Sep 18 the border reads as calm, but an allied judgment of rising limited-attack risk on NATO’s eastern flank makes Belarus’s geography, and its unattributed drone, more consequential than the current lull suggests."}],watch:"Whether the Sep 24 pardons include political prisoners and are tied to announced US sanctions relief; the fate of the potash deal; Polish follow-through on border crossings or the Minsk embassy; any Ukrainian action against Belarusian relay networks; attribution of the Sep 15 Lithuania drone; and any follow-up to Lukashenko’s Sep 11 mobilization remark.",generatedAt:REPORT_NOW.toISOString()};
const NATO_FLANK_INCIDENTS=[
  {date:"Jul 29, 2026",country:"Poland",flag:"🇵🇱",type:"Drone",kind:"incident",color:"#f97316",title:"Drone crashes in eastern Poland",text:"An unmanned aerial vehicle crashed about 50 km from Lublin; PM Donald Tusk said it was ‘probably’ Russian.",src:"Euronews"},
  {date:"Jul 30, 2026",country:"Poland",flag:"🇵🇱",type:"Missile",kind:"incident",color:"#dc2626",title:"Russian cruise missile detonates in Poland",text:"A Russian Kh-101 cruise missile entered Polish airspace during overnight strikes on Ukraine and exploded near the village of Tarnawa-Kolonia, leaving a large crater but no injuries. NATO scrambled F-16s, a tanker and an airborne early-warning aircraft; Rutte called it ‘yet another reckless act by Russia.’",src:"Al Jazeera"},
  {date:"Aug 4, 2026",country:"Germany",flag:"🇩🇪",type:"Drone",kind:"incident",color:"#dc2626",title:"Drone attack at Leipzig/Halle Airport",text:"A drone incident at Leipzig/Halle Airport, which Germany later described as an attempted attack, was formally attributed to Russian state entities on Sep 1.",src:"Stars and Stripes, Jerusalem Post"},
  {date:"Aug 12, 2026",country:"NATO",flag:"🛡️",type:"Assessment",kind:"assessment",color:"#5b8ec8",title:"Allies condemn violations over Poland and Romania",text:"NATO allies said ‘Russia bears full responsibility’ for airspace violations that are ‘dangerous and unacceptable’ and show ‘increasing tolerance for risk,’ and pledged to strengthen integrated air and missile defense.",src:"NATO"},
  {date:"Aug 20, 2026",country:"Romania",flag:"🇷🇴",type:"Maritime drone",kind:"incident",color:"#dc2626",title:"F-16 destroys explosive sea drone near Neptun Deep",text:"A Romanian F-16 destroyed a maritime drone carrying explosives several hundred meters from the Neptun Deep gas project, about 80 nautical miles east of Constanța; the same day another drone crashed in eastern Romania after entering from Ukraine. Ukraine confirmed the sea drone was not Ukrainian.",src:"Euronews"},
  {date:"Aug 31, 2026",country:"Poland",flag:"🇵🇱",type:"Recon intercept",kind:"incident",color:"#eab308",title:"Polish jets intercept Russian Il-20",text:"Polish fighters intercepted a Russian Il-20 reconnaissance aircraft about 30 km north of Łeba; it did not enter Polish airspace. Poland counts it as its seventh interception of Russian military aircraft in 2026.",src:"Militarnyi"},
  {date:"Sep 3, 2026",country:"Poland",flag:"🇵🇱",type:"Recon intercept",kind:"incident",color:"#eab308",title:"Il-20 flies transponder-off near Ustka",text:"Polish jets intercepted another Il-20 about 25 miles north of Ustka flying with its transponder off, outside Polish airspace; Defense Minister Kosiniak-Kamysz announced increased F-16 and helicopter activity.",src:"Stars and Stripes"},
  {date:"Sep 15, 2026",country:"Lithuania",flag:"🇱🇹",type:"Drone",kind:"incident",color:"#dc2626",title:"NATO jets down drone that entered from Belarus",text:"Italian jets on Baltic Air Policing shot down a drone near Pratkūnai, close to Kaunas, after it entered from Belarus; Lithuania’s defense minister said it carried an explosive device. Italy’s defense minister said it ‘most probably came from Russia’; Vilnius said origin would be confirmed from debris.",src:"Defense News"},
  {date:"Sep 15, 2026",country:"Denmark",flag:"🇩🇰",type:"Maritime",kind:"incident",color:"#f97316",title:"Russian frigate fires flares toward Danish helicopter",text:"A Russian frigate fired flares toward a Danish military helicopter in international waters, per Denmark’s armed forces.",src:"Defense News, Copenhagen Post"},
  {date:"Sep 23, 2026",country:"Poland",flag:"🇵🇱",type:"Airspace",kind:"incident",color:"#f97316",title:"Russian Mi-8 enters Polish airspace",text:"A Russian Mi-8 helicopter from Kaliningrad crossed about 300 meters into Poland north of Braniewo for 42 seconds; Poland scrambled fighters and said Russia was ‘once again testing the readiness of our air defence.’",src:"Kyiv Independent, Euronews"},
  {date:"Sep 24, 2026",country:"Romania",flag:"🇷🇴",type:"Drone",kind:"incident",color:"#f97316",title:"Drone crashes near Solca",text:"During a Russian barrage on Ukraine, a small drone spent about four minutes in Romanian airspace and crashed in a forest near Solca, Suceava County, with no casualties; fighters were scrambled but did not engage.",src:"Romanian MoD via Agerpres, Kyiv Post"},
  {date:"Sep 24, 2026",country:"Moldova",flag:"🇲🇩",type:"Drone",kind:"incident",color:"#f97316",title:"At least four drones enter Moldova",text:"At least four drones entered Moldova from the Vinnytsia and Chernivtsi directions, prompting a temporary closure of the country’s northern airspace.",src:"112.ua"},
  {date:"Sep 24, 2026",country:"Denmark",flag:"🇩🇰",type:"Assessment",kind:"assessment",color:"#5b8ec8",title:"Denmark: ‘low but growing’ risk of limited attack",text:"Denmark’s Defence Intelligence Service rated the risk of a limited Russian military attack on a NATO border state ‘low but growing,’ possibly ‘in the coming months,’ and Denmark raised its threat level for destructive cyberattacks from medium to high.",src:"ArcticToday, TechSavvy"}
];
const ESCALATION_RISKS=[
  {theater:"Iran – US / Gulf",icon:"🇮🇷",level:"SEVERE",color:"#dc2626",text:"The only theater with sustained direct combat between a nuclear-armed power's forces and a state adversary. Hormuz blockade, a fortified nuclear-site target under explicit threat, and rising US/Iranian casualties push this to the top of the board."},
  {theater:"Russia – Ukraine / NATO",icon:"🇺🇦",level:"ELEVATED",color:"#f97316",text:"Deep NATO-member materiel and licensing involvement (Patriot co-production, intelligence-cued strikes) without direct NATO-Russia combat. The Aug 19-20 Kyiv barrage struck a Romanian offshore gas project and a Moldovan border crossing the same night — the first same-night spillover into two neighbouring states, one of them a NATO member — Italian jets downed a likely Russian armed drone over Lithuania on Sep 15, a Russian Mi-8 helicopter crossed about 300m into Polish airspace from Kaliningrad on Sep 23, and a drone that entered Romania from Ukraine crashed near Solca on Sep 24 after F-16s were scrambled — yet no ally has requested Article 4 consultations since Estonia in Sep 2025. Nuclear rhetoric remains coercive rather than operational per IC consensus; China's tacit red line is the main restraint on Moscow."},
  {theater:"Israel & the Levant",icon:"🇮🇱",level:"ELEVATED",color:"#f97316",text:"Post-ceasefire violence running at a higher tempo than the truce implies, with Lebanon and Syria fronts both live. Contained to regional actors so far — no direct US or Iranian state-on-state exchange tied to this front specifically."},
  {theater:"Indo-Pacific / Taiwan & SCS",icon:"🇹🇼",level:"MODERATE",color:"#eab308",text:"Grey-zone coercion (militia swarms, live-fire drills, laser/water-cannon incidents) at record frequency, now extending to Japan's Okinotori. No kinetic engagement between PLA and US/allied forces — the gap between friction and war remains wide but is narrowing."},
  {theater:"Americas / Venezuela & Cuba",icon:"🇻🇪",level:"MODERATE",color:"#eab308",text:"A US regional-intervention campaign (Maduro's capture, Cuba oil blockade) rather than a great-power confrontation — Russia's blockade-running tanker to Havana is the closest brush with direct great-power friction in this theater."},
  {theater:"Global Nuclear Threshold",icon:"☢️",level:"MODERATE",color:"#eab308",text:"New START's Feb 2026 lapse leaves the two largest arsenals uncapped for the first time since 1972; Iran's enrichment clock and Belarus-based Russian tactical weapons add live vectors. No state has crossed a use-threshold."},
  {theater:"Direct Great-Power Combat",icon:"⚖️",level:"LOW",color:"#22c55e",text:"No US, Russian, or Chinese regular forces are in direct combat with one another anywhere on the board — the structural line that separates today's multi-theater tension from an actual world war remains, for now, intact."},
];
const ESCALATION_BRIEFING={summaryShort:"Denmark issued the sharpest official NATO-flank warning of the cycle on Sep 24: its Defence Intelligence Service said Russia will attack the West more often and ‘with greater consequences,’ and rated the risk of a limited Russian military attack on a NATO border state ‘low but growing,’ possibly ‘in the coming months’ — via isolated long-range strikes, false-flag use of Ukrainian drones or small troop incursions. The incident record grew too: a Russian Mi-8 crossed 300 meters into Poland for 42 seconds on Sep 23; on Sep 24 at least four drones entered Moldova, which briefly closed its northern airspace, and one crashed near Solca in Romania. No ally has requested Article 4 consultations.",summary:"The NATO–Russia picture now combines a thicker incident log with a formal allied intelligence judgment. On Sep 24 Denmark’s Defence Intelligence Service said Russia will conduct more frequent attacks against the West and NATO ‘with greater consequences for the countries targeted than in the past,’ and assessed a ‘low but growing risk’ that Russia launches a limited military attack on NATO border nations; service chief Thomas Ahrenkiel said such action ‘could potentially take place in the coming months’ and listed isolated long-range strikes on infrastructure supporting Ukraine, false-flag operations with Ukrainian drones, and limited troop deployments under pretexts such as protecting Russian minorities — ArcticToday, Jerusalem Post. The same day Denmark raised its threat level for destructive cyberattacks to high (see Cyber). Earlier this month a Russian frigate fired flares toward a Danish military helicopter in international waters. The incident record: on Sep 23 a Russian Mi-8 from Kaliningrad entered Polish airspace north of Braniewo, about 300 meters deep for 42 seconds; Poland scrambled fighters and called it a test of air-defense readiness. Early Sep 24, during a Russian barrage on Ukraine, a small drone crashed in a forest near Solca, Romania, after about four minutes in Romanian airspace with no casualties; fighters were scrambled but did not engage, which Romania’s defense minister attributed to peacetime rules of engagement. The same morning at least four drones entered Moldova from the Vinnytsia and Chernivtsi directions, and Chișinău temporarily closed its northern airspace; Poland’s Operational Command deployed forces as Russia struck western Ukraine — 112.ua, Kyiv Post. The counter-view remains: Finland’s Stubb called attack predictions an ‘overreaction’ and Estonia’s Tsahkna said the far side of his border is ‘empty’ because Russian troops are in Ukraine. Denmark’s own wording — limited, deniable, low probability but rising — is consistent with both camps. No ally has requested Article 4 consultations since Estonia in Sep 2025, and the Kremlin denies the allegations.",assessment:[{cat:"A Named, Dated Risk Judgment Changes the Debate",text:"Denmark’s ‘low but growing’ assessment with a ‘coming months’ horizon gives Koudelka’s and Tusk’s warnings an official allied intelligence counterpart; it is not a prediction of war, but it narrows the space for dismissing the risk as rhetoric."},{cat:"Spillover Now Touches Three NATO-Adjacent States Per Barrage",text:"Poland, Romania and Moldova all logged incidents within about 24 hours, and the Danish warning explicitly names false-flag use of Ukrainian drones — exactly the ambiguity that stray drones from Russian barrages already create."},{cat:"Rules of Engagement Remain the Exposed Seam",text:"Unengaged drones over Romania and brief helicopter incursions over Poland are cheap tests of alliance response; until capitals change peacetime rules or seek Article 4 consultations, Moscow can keep probing at low cost."}],watch:"Whether other allies echo Denmark’s ‘low but growing’ judgment or adjust force posture; any Article 4 request or change to Polish or Romanian rules of engagement; further drone incursions into Moldova, Romania or Poland during Russian barrages; a Russian response on the Mi-8 incident; and whether the Mediterranean ship-launched drone report is corroborated.",generatedAt:REPORT_NOW.toISOString()};
const CYBER_BRIEFING={summaryShort:"The quiet on the sabotage track ended in the form of a warning, not an incident: on Sep 24 Denmark’s Agency for Civil Security raised its threat level for destructive cyberattacks from medium to high, citing risks to critical infrastructure from wiper and operational-technology attacks, after the PET security service identified ‘concrete Russian planning and preparations for acts of sabotage against Denmark.’ Denmark’s military intelligence separately said Russia’s hybrid attacks will become more frequent and more consequential. No new confirmed arson or intrusion against a European target surfaced in this refresh’s checks; the latest physical events remain the Aug–Sep wave on drone and radar suppliers Milrem, JFG Composites, WB Electronics and Rohde & Schwarz.",summary:"Denmark has turned Europe’s hybrid-threat warnings into a formal posture change. On Sep 24 the Danish Agency for Civil Security raised its threat level for destructive cyberattacks from medium to high, warning of risks to critical national infrastructure, government agencies, businesses and citizens from wiper attacks and manipulation of operational technology; PET director Laila Reenberg said ‘Russia is waging hybrid warfare against the West and is taking on greater risks,’ and the service identified ‘concrete Russian planning and preparations for acts of sabotage against Denmark’ — TechSavvy. The Defence Intelligence Service said the same day that Russia will attack more often ‘with greater consequences for the countries targeted than in the past’ — ArcticToday. Denmark has previously blamed Russia for cyberattacks on a water utility that left homes without water. No new confirmed physical sabotage or cyber intrusion against a European target surfaced in this refresh’s checks. The most recent physical wave targeted Ukraine’s drone supply chain: fires at Estonia’s Milrem Robotics (Aug 15), Poland’s JFG Composites (Aug 30) and WB Electronics (Aug 31), where prosecutors opened a terrorism and foreign-intelligence case, and an arson attempt near Rohde & Schwarz’s Munich headquarters in early September that led to the arrest of two Bulgarian nationals. Germany attributed the August Leipzig/Halle airport drone attack to Russian state entities on Sep 1. On the cyber side, the last large European event remains the Aug 24 pro-Russian DDoS on Norway’s digitization agency. El Mundo’s Sep 23 report of a CIA warning about ship-launched Gerbera drones toward Spain, France or Italy remains uncorroborated. Malcontent News’s 497-incident count is still dated Sep 2.",assessment:[{cat:"A Government Has Now Acted on Specific Intelligence",text:"PET’s reference to ‘concrete’ Russian sabotage planning, paired with a formal medium-to-high threat-level change, is more than rhetoric: it signals Danish officials have case-level indications, and it obliges infrastructure operators to raise their own defenses."},{cat:"Destructive, Not Just Disruptive, Attacks Are the Stated Concern",text:"Naming wipers and operational-technology manipulation shifts the expected cyber threat from DDoS and data theft toward attacks that damage physical systems — the category behind Denmark’s earlier water-utility incident."},{cat:"A Quiet Incident Log May Understate Activity",text:"The absence of new public incidents since early September now sits against an allied service reporting active preparations and trackers that have not updated since Sep 2, so the lull should be read as low visibility rather than low intent."}],watch:"Whether other European agencies raise cyber threat levels after Denmark; any destructive attack on Nordic or Baltic infrastructure; whether PET’s ‘concrete planning’ leads to arrests or named cases; prosecutions in the Milrem, WB Electronics or Rohde & Schwarz cases; and corroboration of the ship-launched drone warning.",generatedAt:REPORT_NOW.toISOString()};
const GREAT_POWERS_WEEKLY={weekLabel:"Week of Sep 19–25",summaryShort:"Xi Jinping’s Sep 24 White House visit set the week: the summit stabilized US–China relations without settling them. Xi pressed Trump to ‘handle the Taiwan question with prudence,’ the ~$14B Taiwan arms package stayed on hold, and the one concrete deliverable — Bessent’s extension of the Busan trade truce to Jan 10, 2027 — moved the critical-minerals deadline rather than resolving it; no nuclear arms-control track emerged. Beneath the diplomacy, the binding US constraint is magazine depth: the DoD Inspector General found the Iran war left ‘strategic inventory shortfalls,’ with Patriot stocks estimated at 759–827 by July, down from 2,330. Allies are adding capacity at the edges — a US–Denmark–Greenland security agreement and Germany’s near-€140B 2027 defense request — while North Korea tested an upgraded hypersonic missile on Sep 20.",summary:"Xi’s first White House visit in over a decade produced atmospherics rather than a settlement. Per Xinhua, he urged Washington to oppose Taiwan independence and handle the issue ‘with prudence,’ and proposed regular military-to-military dialogue and crisis-communication mechanisms; Reuters reported no change in the US Taiwan formulation and no decision on the ~$14B arms package Trump calls ‘a very good negotiating chip.’ Pressure on Taiwan has shifted shape rather than eased: ISW counted 125 PLA ADIZ incursions in August, PLA ships and aircraft have operated on the island’s Pacific-facing side, and China has kept Coast Guard cutters east of Taiwan since June. The Navy’s FY2027 request ($377.5B, within a $1.5T defense ask) launches the ‘Golden Fleet’ — $65.8B for 34 ships including two Virginia-class submarines, plus advance procurement for a Trump-class battleship — but none of it is appropriated, and the stopgap CR to Dec 11 rejected the battleship and multiyear-munitions requests. The DoD Inspector General’s Sep 15 report put Iran-war munitions spending at $22.3B of $33.4B and found ‘strategic inventory shortfalls’; figures cited by Defense News put Patriot stocks at 759–827 by July (from 2,330) and THAAD at 234–278 (from 452). China’s J-36 and J-50 sixth-generation prototypes have flown since December 2024; the F-47 targets a 2028 first flight. Greenland welcomed a new security agreement with the US and Denmark around UNGA, and sources cited by Defense News say the US plans to reopen a Cold War-era base in southern Greenland and open another on the east coast. Germany expects parliament to approve nearly €140B for defense in 2027 and is lobbying to build PAC-3 interceptors at home; the Sep 2 Marles–Hegseth agreement to accelerate force posture at Australian bases still frames AUKUS, all under a US demand that allies reach 5% of GDP. Bessent’s Sep 23 truce extension to Jan 10, 2027 did not specify which Chinese export-control suspensions it covers; China’s licensing system and military end-user ban remain, and Reuters reports rare-earth deliveries still short of commitments. Washington’s Defense Production Act order requires contractors to prove non-adversary sourcing from January 2027, so both sides’ pressure tools now come due in the same month. Seven months after New START expired, the only brake on US and Russian strategic forces is Moscow’s pledge to respect the central limits as long as Washington does. The summit produced no arms-control channel; China’s arsenal, about 620 warheads per SIPRI, remains the fastest-growing, and USNI identifies North Korea’s Sep 20 Wonsan launches as an upgraded Hwasong-11 hypersonic variant.",sections:[{label:"China & Indo-Pacific",text:"Xi’s first White House visit in over a decade produced atmospherics rather than a settlement. Per Xinhua, he urged Washington to oppose Taiwan independence and handle the issue ‘with prudence,’ and proposed regular military-to-military dialogue and crisis-communication mechanisms; Reuters reported no change in the US Taiwan formulation and no decision on the ~$14B arms package Trump calls ‘a very good negotiating chip.’ Pressure on Taiwan has shifted shape rather than eased: ISW counted 125 PLA ADIZ incursions in August, PLA ships and aircraft have operated on the island’s Pacific-facing side, and China has kept Coast Guard cutters east of Taiwan since June."},{label:"Military Balance",text:"The Navy’s FY2027 request ($377.5B, within a $1.5T defense ask) launches the ‘Golden Fleet’ — $65.8B for 34 ships including two Virginia-class submarines, plus advance procurement for a Trump-class battleship — but none of it is appropriated, and the stopgap CR to Dec 11 rejected the battleship and multiyear-munitions requests. The DoD Inspector General’s Sep 15 report put Iran-war munitions spending at $22.3B of $33.4B and found ‘strategic inventory shortfalls’; figures cited by Defense News put Patriot stocks at 759–827 by July (from 2,330) and THAAD at 234–278 (from 452). China’s J-36 and J-50 sixth-generation prototypes have flown since December 2024; the F-47 targets a 2028 first flight."},{label:"Alliances & Posture",text:"Greenland welcomed a new security agreement with the US and Denmark around UNGA, and sources cited by Defense News say the US plans to reopen a Cold War-era base in southern Greenland and open another on the east coast. Germany expects parliament to approve nearly €140B for defense in 2027 and is lobbying to build PAC-3 interceptors at home; the Sep 2 Marles–Hegseth agreement to accelerate force posture at Australian bases still frames AUKUS, all under a US demand that allies reach 5% of GDP."},{label:"Resources & Industry",text:"Bessent’s Sep 23 truce extension to Jan 10, 2027 did not specify which Chinese export-control suspensions it covers; China’s licensing system and military end-user ban remain, and Reuters reports rare-earth deliveries still short of commitments. Washington’s Defense Production Act order requires contractors to prove non-adversary sourcing from January 2027, so both sides’ pressure tools now come due in the same month."},{label:"Nuclear Posture",text:"Seven months after New START expired, the only brake on US and Russian strategic forces is Moscow’s pledge to respect the central limits as long as Washington does. The summit produced no arms-control channel; China’s arsenal, about 620 warheads per SIPRI, remains the fastest-growing, and USNI identifies North Korea’s Sep 20 Wonsan launches as an upgraded Hwasong-11 hypersonic variant."}],assessment:[{cat:"Taiwan Is Now Openly Part of the Bargain",tab:"chinaindopac",tabLabel:"China & Indo-Pacific",text:"Trump’s framing of the arms package as a negotiating chip, and Xi’s direct push on it, tie Taiwan’s arms pipeline to the trade track; with no decision taken, Taipei’s exposure runs at least to the Jan 10 truce deadline and the Trump–Xi meetings expected at APEC and the G20."},{cat:"Magazines, Not Hulls, Now Limit Deterrence",tab:"hardware",tabLabel:"Military Balance",text:"The Iran war drew down interceptor and strike-missile stocks faster than industry can replace them; until multiyear munitions funding is appropriated, new ships and aircraft add less deterrent value than restocked magazines would."},{cat:"US Posture Is Growing at the Edges While Costs Shift to Allies",tab:"alliances",tabLabel:"Alliances & Posture",text:"Greenland basing and accelerated Australian posture add US access in the Arctic and Indo-Pacific, while the 5%-of-GDP demand presses allies — Indo-Pacific partners most immediately — to carry more of the bill."},{cat:"The Truce Clock Moved; the Leverage Did Not",tab:"industry",tabLabel:"Resources & Industry",text:"Pushing the deadline to Jan 10 removes the November cliff-edge but leaves Beijing’s licensing architecture intact, and lands its next leverage point within days of Washington’s own January 2027 sourcing deadline."},{cat:"Arms Control Rests on a Conditional Russian Pledge",tab:"nuclear",tabLabel:"Nuclear Posture",text:"With no treaty, no verification and no US–China nuclear channel, restraint depends on a Russian moratorium either side can abandon without a legal trigger, while China’s buildup continues outside any agreement."}],watch:"Whether the ~$14B Taiwan arms package is released, trimmed or delayed after the summit; whether Xi’s proposed military dialogue becomes a standing crisis-communication mechanism; the fate of the Golden Fleet and multiyear-munitions requests after the Dec 11 CR; the published terms of the Greenland agreement; whether MOFCOM formally extends its export-control suspensions to Jan 10; and whether Lavrov’s Sep 26 UN address signals any change to Russia’s conditional adherence to New START limits.",generatedAt:"2026-09-25T07:00:00Z"};
